WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.130
Your chatbot has a computer.

2
00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:08.070
When ChatGPT first launched,
a chatbot was just a large language model.

3
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.890
You typed text in, and it wrote text back.

4
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.400
Today, chatbots like Claude and Copilot
put the language model inside a harness

5
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.580
of software and tools
that includes web search

6
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.090
and often a computer in the cloud,

7
00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:26.010
where it can run code,
make files and analyse data.

8
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.840
Depending on how sophisticated
the harness is,

9
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.230
it can even test what it's made

10
00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.050
and fix any problems
before showing you the result.

11
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:37.040
That's how a chatbot can make
an MP4 video entirely from code,

12
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.810
like the animation you're watching now.

13
00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.410
For this and many other reasons,

14
00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.100
the harness is often more important
than the model in many cases.

15
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.050
Try this: pick Claude Opus 5.5
in both Claude and Copilot,

16
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.320
and give each the same request.

17
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.540
Ask for a 20-second animated video
on a topic of your choice,

18
00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.280
as an MP4 file you can download,

19
00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.010
and ask it to check the video plays
before it shares it.

20
00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.280
Then compare what you get back.

21
00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.040
Same model. Different harness.

22
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.670
You'll find the full prompt
and links to further reading in the post.
